      {
         "id": "SECTION_introduction",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Introduction\n\nIn the previous chapter, we explored how counting developed and how it helps us in everyday life. Now, let us discover another skill essential to both daily life and science: the act of comparing.\n\nThink about it for a moment:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 1: Comparing</b><br /><br />Are two red apples the same?<br /><br />What about two cars of the same model but different colors? </div></div></center></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Often, we classify things as equal because they look alike, or we see them as completely different when, in reality, they share many traits. In this chapter, we will examine these situations and better understand what it truly means to compare.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_initial",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "<p><h3>What Does It Mean to Be Equal?</h3>Before we start comparing, it is important to define the concept of equality. To do so, we highlight a relevant definition of the word <i>equal</i> from the Britannica Dictionary (<htcite0>):</p><p><ul class=\"citation\"> <li>\"<i>B1. the same in amount, number, or size.</i>\"</li></ul></p><p>With this definition in mind, look at <b>Chart 1</b>:</p><p><div class=\"htTwoGeometricalRectangle\"> <div class=\"htFirstGeometrical\"><div class=\"htCircle\" style=\"height: 12vh; width: 12vh;\"></div></div> <div class=\"htSecondGeometrical\"><div class=\"htCircle\" style=\"height: 12vh; width: 12vh;\"></div></div> </div></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Chart 1</b>: Two identical circles.</p><p>In <b>Chart 1</b>, we see two circles that perfectly match the first definition — they are identical in every characteristic. Now, let us compare them with <b>Chart 2</b>:</p><p><div class=\"htTwoGeometricalRectangle\"> <div class=\"htFirstGeometrical\"><div class=\"htCircle\" style=\"height: 12vh; width: 12vh;\"></div></div> <div class=\"htSecondGeometrical\"><div class=\"htCircle\" style=\"height: 12vh; width: 12vh; background-color: blue;\"></div></div> </div></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Chart 2</b>: Two similar circles, but with different colors.</p><p>In <b>Chart 2</b>, the two circles are identical in every way except for their color. Thus, while they are similar (<htcite1>), they are not exactly the same.</p><p><center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 2: On Groups</b><br /><br />Does changing the color of an object change its type?</div></div></center></p><p>To answer this question, we first need to define what a circle is. According to Eric W. Weisstein's definition (<htcite2>):</p><p><div class=\"desc\"><div class=\"circle2\" style=\"width:12vh;height:12vh;display: flex;align-items: center;justify-content: center;\"><div class=\"circle\" style=\"width:1vh;height:1vh;background-color:black\"></div></div></div></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Chart 3</b>: A circle used in the mathematical definition.</p><p><ul class=\"citation\"> <li>\"<i>A circle is the set of points in a plane that are equidistant from a given central point.</i>\"</li></ul></p><p>Notice that the definition of a circle does not mention color as an essential characteristic. Therefore, although the circles in <b>Chart 2</b> differ in color, they still retain their main properties and continue to be circles.</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 0,
                     "uuid": "425c72c4-6286-44cf-80e9-d9b21d0735ea",
                     "text": "The Britannica Dictionary",
